+# Why production lands on mainnet.base.org
+
+## TL;DR
+
+`NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` is **inlined at build time**, not read at runtime. The
+static production bundle was compiled in an environment where that var was
+**not set**, so `rpcOverrides[base.id]` baked into the JS as `undefined`. With
+no override, wagmi's transport calls bare `http()`, which viem resolves to the
+chain's default public RPC — `https://mainnet.base.org` — and it gets
+rate-limited (429). Devs never see it because their `.env.local` sets the var,
+so their local build inlines the paid URL.
+
+---
+
+## The exact chain
+
+### 1. `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` is a build-time text substitution
+
+In Next.js, any `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` reference is **not** an environment lookup in
+the browser. During `next build`, Next.js does a literal find-and-replace:
+every `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` in the source is swapped for the string
+literal that was present in the build shell at compile time. The browser has no
+`process.env`; there is nothing to read at runtime.
+
+For a **static export on generic hosting** this is terminal: there is no Node
+server, so setting the variable in the hosting dashboard as a *runtime* env var
+does nothing. Only the value present when the bundle was compiled matters.
+
+### 2. Where the value diverges
+
+- **Dev machine:** `packages/nextjs/.env.local` contains
+  `NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL=<paid RPC>`. `next dev` / `next build` inlines the paid
+  URL. No 429s — ever.
+- **CI / hosting build:** `.env.local` is gitignored and absent, and the var
+  wasn't exported into the **build** step (it may be set as a *runtime*
+  dashboard var, which a static export can't use). So
+  `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` inlines to `undefined`. The shipped bundle
+  literally contains `rpcOverrides: { 8453: undefined }`.
+
+### 3. `scaffold.config.ts`
+
+```ts
+rpcOverrides: { [chains.base.id]: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL } // → { 8453: undefined } in prod
+```
+
+### 4. `wagmiConfig.tsx` — the `client({ chain })` factory
+
+```ts
+let rpcFallbacks = [http()];                         // default = public RPC
+const rpcOverrideUrl = scaffoldConfig.rpcOverrides?.[chain.id];
+if (rpcOverrideUrl) {
+  rpcFallbacks = [http(rpcOverrideUrl), http()];     // paid first, public still present
+} else {
+  const alchemyHttpUrl = getAlchemyHttpUrl(chain.id);
+  if (alchemyHttpUrl) {
+    const isUsingDefaultKey = scaffoldConfig.alchemyApiKey === DEFAULT_ALCHEMY_API_KEY;
+    rpcFallbacks = isUsingDefaultKey ? [http(), http(alchemyHttpUrl)] : [http(alchemyHttpUrl), http()];
+  }
+}
+return createClient({ chain, transport: fallback(rpcFallbacks), ... });
+```
+
+In prod, `rpcOverrideUrl` is falsy → the `else` branch. With no per-app Alchemy
+key for Base (or the default SE-2 key), `rpcFallbacks` stays/falls to
+**`[http()]`** (and if the default Alchemy key is set, public `http()` is
+*first* anyway).
+
+### 5. What bare `http()` does
+
+viem's `http()` **with no URL** uses `chain.rpcUrls.default.http[0]`. For Base
+that is `https://mainnet.base.org`. Every JSON-RPC call in production (polling,
+reads, `useScaffold*` hooks) fires at the public endpoint → 429.
+
+**Chain:** unset build-env var → `NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` inlines `undefined` →
+`rpcOverrides[8453]=undefined` → `rpcOverrideUrl` falsy → `rpcFallbacks=[http()]`
+→ `http()` resolves to `mainnet.base.org` → rate-limited.
+
+---
+
+## The one operational check that settles it
+
+**Don't check the dashboard env var — inspect what's actually in the shipped
+bundle / on the wire.** Because the URL is build-time inlined, the only ground
+truth is the compiled output:
+
+> Open the **production** site → DevTools → Network → filter the JSON-RPC POSTs
+> and confirm the host is the paid RPC, not `mainnet.base.org`. Equivalently:
+> `grep -r "mainnet.base.org"` (and the paid host) in the deployed
+> `_next/static` JS.
+
+If the paid URL is baked in and requests go there, the fix is real. If
+`mainnet.base.org` still appears in the bundle or in the prod Network tab, it
+isn't — regardless of what the hosting dashboard shows. This also proves the
+real fix: set `NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` in the **build** environment (CI/build step)
+and **rebuild**; redeploying the same artifact or flipping a runtime var changes
+nothing.
+
+---
+
+## The second, transport-level trap (survives fixing the env var)
+
+Even once the override is correctly inlined, the override branch builds:
+
+```ts
+rpcFallbacks = [http(rpcOverrideUrl), http()];   // ← bare http() still in the list
+```
+
+and wraps it in `fallback(...)`. viem's `fallback` transport **advances to the
+next transport on any error** from the primary — a 429, a timeout, a 5xx, a
+transient network blip on the paid RPC. That next transport is bare `http()` =
+`mainnet.base.org`. So under exactly the conditions where the paid RPC is
+stressed, *some* production requests silently spill back onto the public
+endpoint — the very endpoint you were trying to avoid — and it's invisible
+because the call still succeeds.
+
+**Fix:** drop the public fallback when an override exists, e.g.
+`rpcFallbacks = [http(rpcOverrideUrl)]` (or plain `transport: http(rpcOverrideUrl)`).
+Keep a fallback only if you deliberately accept public-RPC spillover.
+
+---
+
+### Aside (related, chains-level, not the asked trap)
+
+`enabledChains` always injects Ethereum **mainnet** (chain 1) for ENS/price.
+`rpcOverrides` only covers `base.id`, so every ENS/ETH-price call goes through
+bare `http()` on chain 1 → Ethereum's default public RPC regardless of the Base
+fix. Different public endpoint, same class of leak; add a `[chains.mainnet.id]`
+override if that traffic matters.

